Aut and his siblings are not gods. [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anaïs Marchal|Anaïs Marchal]] calls them the archetypal parts of a book, and each of them holds a piece of how a story runs.

[[Aut]] is the Writer, who designs the path he thinks the story should take. [[Divinity/The Narrative/Renge|Renge]] is the Fickle Mistress, the things that might happen instead. [[Divinity/The Narrative/Petr|Petr]] is the Threshold a hero crosses. [[Divinity/The Narrative/Insuperlex|Insuperlex]] is the Mountain set in a hero's way, and [[Divinity/The Narrative/Menhir|the Menhir]] was the Heroes themselves. The Menhir is dead, killed with the weapon it had given the Party, and four of the five remain.

Aut and Renge are at odds because their domains overlap. Insuperlex and the Menhir clashed the same way. To Renge, anything too powerful to be overcome is a balance breaker, and no story she cares about has room for one. The siblings keep no council and work together only when they must, and no one who leads a real [[Org/index|Org]] is a friend to any of them.

Their rules are not the rules that bind mortals, or even gods. They are empowered to act the further events stray from the plot they mean the story to follow, though what that plot is supposed to be is not known. They would tell you they are omnipotent, which is not true, and when all of them turn their eyes to one thing at once, that thing is the only thing they are watching. Those who serve them aboard the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]] believe three of the five have taken an interest in the pilgrimage, and likely all five.
